{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210730,"nonce":"hNazr/qdoAuZSWfaWMlomJuFMD4=","title":"JIT'd constructor body ran with caller's module_vars","message":"Fixed in commit 9384a57. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n`call_constructor_sync_impl` (vm.rs) updates `JitContext.{current_func_id, closure, defining_class}` before invoking a JIT'd constructor body, but never refreshes `module_vars` / `module_name` to the callee's module. Every other JIT entry point already does this swap (`call_jit_with_shadow`, `Method::Closure` JIT-leaf path in BC interp, `dispatch_closure_bc_inner` threaded path); the constructor path was the lone holdout.\n\n**Why:** A `main` function calling `Camera2D.new(960, 720)` (constructor in `@hatch:gpu`) runs the JIT'd body with main's slot table still in `module_vars`. The body's `_origin = Vec3.new(0, 0, 0)` resolves `Vec3` via `GetModuleVar @N` against main's slots — main doesn't import `Vec3`, so the slot is undefined and the call returns null. `_origin` ends up null. Reproduces every time on `@hatch:game`'s ECS example: BC-interp'd first call works, second call (after tier-up) fails because main never imports the gpu-internal `Vec3`.\n\n**How to apply:** Fixed in commit 9384a57. Look up `engine.func_module(func_id)` for the callee's defining module, find its `Module.vars` slice, publish base/len/name/name-len into JitContext alongside the existing func_id/closure/defining_class writes. Restore the caller's context via the existing `set_jit_context(saved_ctx)` after the call. Any new JIT entry point added later that takes a closure or constructor pointer must do the same module-vars swap or risk the same silent cross-module null.","files":null}]}